research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11886-+{"id":"cor-topological-groups-are-completely-regular","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Uniformizability of every topological group and complete regularity of uniformizable spaces are standard, but the item states their precise DC-dependent composition under the non-Hausdorff convention. No source states that exact package. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Every topological group carries its canonical uniformities, and uniformizable spaces are completely regular in the library's separation-free convention. Their composition, with DC explicitly exposed for the latter implication, is a standard corollary rather than a novel theorem.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11887- {"id":"def-pointwise-and-uniform-convergence-uniformities","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"BIU defines pointwise convergence from finite coordinate restrictions and uniform convergence from the full coordinate set. The item writes explicit equivalent bases P(F,V) and Q(V).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11888- {"id":"lem-uniform-convergence-uniformity-is-finer-than-pointwise","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"BIU gives the two bases. Their inclusion and equality when the domain itself is finite are the immediate standard comparison isolated here.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11889- {"id":"ex-usual-metric-uniformity-on-r","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The metric-uniformity construction specializes to the usual absolute-value metric on R and gives its topology and epsilon-delta uniform continuity. The item is that standard specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log:11890:-{"id":"ex-r-and-open-interval-homeomorphic-not-uniformly-isomorphic","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The homeomorphism h(x)=x/(1+|x|) is a standard bounded-coordinate example; searches found only informal exact discussions, not an allowed source for this witness. The verification shows established mathematics, not novelty. Its triangle-inequality citation is wrong.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11891--{"id":"fs-a-compatible-uniformity-is-unique","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Nonuniqueness of compatible uniformities on noncompact spaces is standard; Wodzicki proves uniqueness only compactly and gives continuous-not-uniformly-continuous witnesses. No source states this exact false universal plus pullback refutation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11892--{"id":"ex-additive-group-uniformity-on-r","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf","https://homepages.math.uic.edu/~rosendal/PapersWebsite/Coarse-Geometry-Book23.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The sourced group formulas and commutativity make all four group uniformities equal the usual metric uniformity on additive R. No source states this exact four-way example, but it is standard, not novel.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11893--{"id":"ex-pointwise-but-not-uniform-convergence-on-a-countable-domain","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Moving-tail indicator functions are a canonical pointwise-not-uniform witness, but no reputable source with this exact N-to-{0,1} construction surfaced. The verification is direct and established; its discrete-uniformity citation is incomplete.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log:11894:+{"id":"ex-r-and-open-interval-homeomorphic-not-uniformly-isomorphic","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The homeomorphism h(x)=x/(1+|x|) is a standard bounded-coordinate example; searches found only informal exact discussions, not an allowed source for this witness. The verification shows established mathematics, not novelty. Its triangle-inequality citation is wrong. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: The bounded-coordinate homeomorphism x/(1+|x|), its explicit inverse, and the inverse's failure of uniform continuity near the endpoints form a canonical metric/uniform-space example. The current witness is standard and directly recoverable, not AI-invented.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11895-+{"id":"fs-a-compatible-uniformity-is-unique","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Nonuniqueness of compatible uniformities on noncompact spaces is standard; Wodzicki proves uniqueness only compactly and gives continuous-not-uniformly-continuous witnesses. No source states this exact false universal plus pullback refutation. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: A topology need not determine a unique compatible uniformity, and pulling back the interval's metric uniformity along the canonical homeomorphism gives the standard counterexample mechanism. The local false-statement package is established mathematics.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11896-+{"id":"ex-additive-group-uniformity-on-r","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf","https://homepages.math.uic.edu/~rosendal/PapersWebsite/Coarse-Geometry-Book23.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The sourced group formulas and commutativity make all four group uniformities equal the usual metric uniformity on additive R. No source states this exact four-way example, but it is standard, not novel. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: In an abelian topological group the left and right uniformities coincide, so their upper join and Roelcke meet coincide as well; on additive R the common entourages are exactly the usual metric ones. This is a canonical worked instance of standard definitions.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11897-+{"id":"ex-pointwise-but-not-uniform-convergence-on-a-countable-domain","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Moving-tail indicator functions are a canonical pointwise-not-uniform witness, but no reputable source with this exact N-to-{0,1} construction surfaced. The verification is direct and established; its discrete-uniformity citation is incomplete. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Moving-tail indicator functions on N are a canonical example separating pointwise from uniform convergence: every coordinate is eventually zero while each function still attains one. The zero-one target uniformity makes the standard witness exact.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11898- {"id":"ex-closed-unit-interval-has-one-compatible-uniformity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Vienna Theorem 9.4.14 gives the unique compatible uniformity on every compact Hausdorff space, and the metric construction identifies it on [0,1]. The item is this standard specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-alpha-d2-recovery.log-11899--{"id":"cex-k-topology-is-not-uniformizable","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://faculty.etsu.edu/gardnerr/5357/notes/Munkres-31.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. ETSU notes prove the K-topology Hausdorff but not regular, while uniformizable spaces are regular. Their composition gives this conclusion, but no source states it as one proposition; it is standard, not novel.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
